+## Audit Hub
+
+The project ships with the connector of Audit Hub, a separate service that keeps a tamper-evident
+log of its security events, alerts on them and answers "my account's security"
+(`apps/api/src/audit/`, `apps/web/src/audit/`). **It is off** until every one of
+`AUDIT_HUB_URL`, `AUDIT_HUB_APP`, `AUDIT_HUB_KEY_ID`, `AUDIT_HUB_KEY_SECRET` and
+`AUDIT_HUB_PSEUDONYM_KEY` is set ([environment.md](environment.md#api-variables)): then nothing
+is recorded or sent, its routes answer 404, and the boot is unaffected.
+
+When it is on:
+
+- **What is recorded:** the flows above (sign-ins and their failures, sign-outs, session theft,
+  password and email changes, sign-ups, invitations, role changes, disabled accounts, required
+  password changes, deletions, admins reading other people's records, cross-origin refusals and
+  rate-limited auth requests), each in the transaction of its change (an outbox), so a change
+  that rolls back sends nothing. Each one's point in the code is listed in Audit Hub's connection
+  guide (`docs/audit/connection-guide.md` in its repository).
+- **What never leaves:** a password, a token or a secret; an email leaves only as a keyed
+  pseudonym (HMAC with `AUDIT_HUB_PSEUDONYM_KEY`) and a masked label. The address of a request is
+  the one `TRUST_PROXY` gives ([rate limiting](#rate-limiting)), so a forged `X-Forwarded-For`
+  changes nothing.
+- **What Audit Hub can ask** (signed webhooks, `AUDIT_HUB_WEBHOOK_SECRET`): "Was it you?" after
+  an unusual sign-in (a "no" ends every session of the account), and the response actions the
+  project lists in `AUDIT_HUB_RESPONSE_ACTIONS` (`revoke_sessions`, `require_password_change`,
+  `disable_account`), which do what an admin does here, under the same rules (the last admin
+  who may sign in is never disabled). Each outcome is recorded.
+- **The browser** reports, through a lazy chunk loaded only when the capabilities say so
+  (`auditClientEvents`): a Content Security Policy violation (its directive and the blocked
+  origin, never a path), a refused way back after sign-in, a session the server ended. The
+  Account page lists the person's own activity, which Audit Hub returns without addresses.
+- **Deleting an account** tells Audit Hub, which then erases the person's personal data it holds.
+- **Privacy:** the project is the controller of these data, and Audit Hub's operator processes
+  them on its behalf. The project's privacy notice says so: Audit Hub's repository has the
+  paragraphs, the processor contract and the procedure for rights requests (`docs/privacy/`).
+
+When Audit Hub cannot be reached the events wait in the outbox, nothing is lost; the alerts and
+the runbook: [incident-response.md](incident-response.md#17-audit-hub-connector-behind).
+
